<h2>Three days between a death and an FIR</h2>
<p>The timeline troubled the Court before anything else did. The hospital informed the police of a suspected unnatural death by a daily diary entry on 4 December 2022. The FIR, however, was registered only on 7 December 2022 at 8:15 in the evening — and the record offered no explanation for those three days. What followed was the opposite extreme: the investigation was, on the prosecution record itself, substantially completed within hours, by the midnight of 7/8 December 2022. An investigation that sleeps for three days and then concludes overnight invites exactly the scrutiny it received here.</p>

<h2>The evidence, weighed at the bail standard</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Last-seen witness</strong><p>The witness did not know the accused, no test identification parade was held, and the first identification came in the witness box during trial — years after the incident.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Forensic link</strong><p>The FSL report found the deceased's DNA on the ligature. That proves the ligature was used in the crime; it does not connect the accused to the ligature.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Arrest record</strong><p>When the Court asked about the circumstances of the arrest, the investigating officer failed to produce the arrest memo.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Custody and progress</strong><p>The accused had spent nearly four years in custody. All public witnesses stood examined, so the tampering apprehension had thinned considerably.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Why these defects mattered at this stage</h2>
<p>A bail court does not conduct a mini-trial, but it does take a hard look at the quality of the material said to justify detention. Each strand of the prosecution case had a structural weakness. The identification evidence suffered from the absence of a test identification parade — a safeguard whose whole purpose is to test a stranger-witness's memory before the trial supplies him the face in the dock. The scientific evidence established the instrument of the offence but not the hand that wielded it. And the unexplained FIR delay, coupled with the missing arrest memo, went to the integrity of the investigation itself. Taken together with almost four years of incarceration and a trial that had examined only 10 of 25 witnesses, the balance tilted decisively towards liberty.</p>

<h2>The operative order</h2>
<p>Bail was granted on a personal bond of Rs. 20,000 with one surety in the like amount, to the satisfaction of the trial court or the Duty Magistrate, with a copy of the order sent immediately to the Jail Superintendent. The Court was careful to record that its assessment was limited to the bail question and that the trial court must assess the evidence independently at the final stage — the standard insulation that keeps a bail order from casting a shadow on the trial.</p>

<h2>What the order signals for bail practice</h2>
<p>For those advising an accused in a case resting on last-seen testimony, the order underlines three points of attack that recur in Delhi bail practice. First, reconstruct the FIR timeline from the daily diary entries: unexplained gaps between information and registration are taken seriously, because they open the door to deliberation and embellishment. Second, where the identifying witness is a stranger to the accused, the absence of a test identification parade is not a technicality — it degrades the identification to a near-worthless dock recognition. Third, forensic reports must be read for what they actually prove: DNA on an object connects the object to the crime, not the accused to the object. None of this acquits anyone; all of it bears on whether continued detention pending a slow-moving trial is justified. The offence in this case was registered under Section 302 of the Indian Penal Code, the provision corresponding to Section 103 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 for offences committed after 1 July 2024.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This article summarises a judicial order for general information. The order in BAIL APPLN. 3281/2026 decides only the question of bail and records no finding on guilt. The trial in FIR No. 718/2022, PS Mandawali, continues before the trial court.</p>
</div>
